<!-- ===================== SECTION 4: MAHAJANAPADAS & MAGADHA EMPIRE ===================== -->
<h2 id="mahajanapadas">4. Mahajanapadas & Rise of Magadha (600-321 BCE)</h2>

<p>The 6th century BCE was a transformative period in Indian history - the <span class="highlight">Second Urbanisation</span>, marked by the rise of territorial states, coinage, trade routes, the <span class="highlight">shramana</span> (renunciant) movements that rejected Vedic orthodoxy, and new philosophical systems. Buddhist and Jain texts mention <strong>16 Mahajanapadas</strong> ('great territorial states') that existed in northern India from the 6th to 4th centuries BCE. These represented the transition from the tribal jana to the territorial janapada - people were now identified by their region rather than their clan. This period saw the emergence of complex state structures, professional armies, regular taxation, written records, and the first imperial ambitions.</p>

<h3>The 16 Mahajanapadas</h3>

<p>The <strong>Anguttara Nikaya</strong> (Buddhist text) and the <strong>Bhagavati Sutra</strong> (Jain text) list the 16 Mahajanapadas. They were distributed across the Indus-Gangetic plain and surrounding regions. Political organisation varied - some were monarchies (rajya/rajadhiraja), others were <span class="highlight">gana-sanghas</span> (republics/oligarchies, also called an-rajya). The most powerful kingdoms were Kosala, Magadha, and Vatsa; the strongest gana-sanghas were the Vrijjis (Lichchhavis), Mallas, and Shakya (the Buddha's own clan).</p>

<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Mahajanapada</th><th>Capital</th><th>Modern Region</th><th>Type</th><th>Key Features</th></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Magadha</strong></td><td>Girivraja (Rajgir), later Pataliputra</td><td>South Bihar</td><td>Monarchy</td><td>Most powerful; iron ore; Ganga river access; later became the Mauryan heartland</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Kosala</strong></td><td>Shravasti (Sahet-Mahet)</td><td>Eastern Uttar Pradesh</td><td>Monarchy</td><td>King Prasenajit; Saketa and Ayodhya also important; Mahavira and Buddha both taught here</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Kuru</strong></td><td>Indraprastha, Hastinapur</td><td>Haryana/Delhi</td><td>Monarchy</td><td>Kuru kingdom declined but remained 'cultural heartland' of Vedic orthodoxy</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Panchala</strong></td><td>Ahichchhatra (north), Kampilya (south)</td><td>Western Uttar Pradesh</td><td>Monarchy</td><td>King Pravahana Jaivali mentioned in Chandogya Upanishad; centre of Vedic learning</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Vatsa</strong></td><td>Kaushambi (Allahabad region)</td><td>Eastern U.P. near Allahabad</td><td>Monarchy</td><td>King Udayana, a contemporary of Buddha; important trade centre on Yamuna; cosmopolitan city</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Avanti</strong></td><td>Ujjayini (north), Mahishmati (south)</td><td>Malwa (M.P.)</td><td>Monarchy</td><td>King Chanda Pradyota; trade route from north to south; later conquered by Magadha</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Gandhara</strong></td><td>Takshashila (Taxila)</td><td>Peshawar region (Pakistan)</td><td>Monarchy</td><td>King Pukkusati; famous university of Taxila; gateway for Persian/Hellenistic influence</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Kamboja</strong></td><td>Rajapura?</td><td>Northeast Afghanistan/Kashmir</td><td>Republic</td><td>Horse-breeding region (ashva-yuddha-kushala - skilled in horse-warfare); Indo-Iranian</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Anga</strong></td><td>Champa (Bhagalpur)</td><td>East Bihar</td><td>Monarchy</td><td>Conquered by Magadha's King Bimbisara; wealthy trading port on the Ganga</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Matsya</strong></td><td>Viratnagar (Bairat)</td><td>Jaipur region (Rajasthan)</td><td>Monarchy</td><td>Small kingdom; traditionally associated with the Pandavas' exile in the Mahabharata</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Shurasena</strong></td><td>Mathura</td><td>Western U.P. (Mathura)</td><td>Monarchy</td><td>Krishna's clan (Yadava); Mathura became a major Buddhist and Jain centre; later Kushan capital</td></tr>
